---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { LINKS, NAME, OWNER } from "../lib/site";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const link = "font-medium text-fg underline decoration-accent underline-offset-4";
---

<Base title={`Terms · ${NAME}`} description="The terms for using GrooveKit and this website.">
  <article class="container-site max-w-3xl py-24 sm:py-32">
    <p class="eyebrow">Terms</p>
    <h1 class="title mt-4">Free to use. Your music is yours.</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-faint">Last updated 16 November 2026</p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10 leading-relaxed text-dim [&_h2]:mb-3 [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:text-fg [&_li]:mt-2 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5">
      <section>
        <h2>Who these terms are with</h2>
        <p>
          GrooveKit and groovekit.org are published by {OWNER.name}, Vienna, Austria (see the{" "}
          <a href={`${base}/imprint/`} class={link}>imprint</a>). These terms apply when you download or use GrooveKit
          or use this website. The <a href={`${base}/privacy/`} class={link}>privacy page</a> explains what happens to
          your data.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The license</h2>
        <p>
          GrooveKit is free and open-source software under the{" "}
          <a href={LINKS.license} class={link}>GNU Affero General Public License v3</a> (AGPLv3). You can use it for any
          purpose, private or commercial, at no cost, and study, change and share its source code under that license.
          If you pass on GrooveKit or a changed version, or let others use a changed version over a network, the AGPLv3
          asks you to offer its source code under the same license. Where these terms and the AGPLv3 differ about the
          software itself, the AGPLv3 applies. The third-party software in GrooveKit stays under its own licenses,
          listed in the app under Settings → About → Licenses.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          The name GrooveKit and its logo belong to {OWNER.name}. The license doesn't cover them: a changed version you
          share should go by a name of its own.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What you make is yours</h2>
        <p>
          {OWNER.name} claims no rights to the songs, recordings, mixes, masters and other work you make with GrooveKit,
          and the AGPLv3 doesn't apply to them. You can release, sell and license them however you like.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The <a href={LINKS.starter} class={link}>starter samples</a> GrooveKit offers to download, and the sounds
            built into its own instruments, are public domain (CC0 or Unlicense), so you can use them in your music
            without asking or crediting anyone.
          </li>
          <li>
            For everything you bring in yourself (samples, loops, reference tracks, recordings and plugins) you're
            responsible for having the right to use it as you do. Reference tracks are only analysed and listened to,
            on your Mac; using them doesn't give you any right to their music.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The agent</h2>
        <p>
          GrooveKit's agent runs on an AI model you connect yourself: in the app with your own Anthropic API key, or
          through Claude Code or another MCP client. That use is between you and the provider, under its terms and
          prices; {OWNER.name} doesn't sell, resell or pay for it. The agent changes your song only through the same
          steps you could take yourself, and every change can be undone, but it can misjudge what you meant or what
          sounds good. Listen to what it did, and keep backups of work that matters.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Models and downloads</h2>
        <p>
          The listening and speech models GrooveKit downloads when you ask for them come from their own publishers,
          under their own licenses (listed in the app). Downloads come from GitHub, where {OWNER.name} publishes the app
          and the starter samples, and from Hugging Face for the models.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>No warranty</h2>
        <p>
          GrooveKit is given away for free, as it is, without any warranty that it works without errors, fits a
          particular purpose or keeps your files safe, as the AGPLv3 says. There's no right to support, updates or
          particular features. Bug reports and feature requests are welcome as{" "}
          <a href={LINKS.newIssue} class={link}>issues</a>, and {OWNER.name} may change, pause or stop GrooveKit and
          this website at any time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Liability</h2>
        <p>
          {OWNER.name} is liable without limit for damage it causes on purpose or through gross negligence, for injury
          to life, body or health, and wherever the law doesn't allow a limit, including under product liability law.
          Otherwise, because GrooveKit is free, {OWNER.name} isn't liable for slight negligence, and in particular not
          for lost work, lost data or lost income. Save your work and keep backups.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>This website</h2>
        <p>
          Texts, images and screenshots on groovekit.org are © {OWNER.name} unless stated otherwise; you may link to
          them and quote them with a credit, but not otherwise reuse them without written consent. The website's source
          code is part of the GrooveKit repository and under the AGPLv3 too. Links to other websites are theirs to
          answer for.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Changes and law</h2>
        <p>
          These terms may change as GrooveKit does; the date above shows when they last did. Austrian law applies,
          without its conflict-of-law rules and the UN Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods. If
          you're a consumer, the mandatory protections of the country you live in still apply, and you can bring a claim
          there. Otherwise the courts of Vienna, Austria, decide. If part of these terms doesn't hold, the rest still
          does. Questions go to <a href={`mailto:${OWNER.email}`} class={link}>{OWNER.email}</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
